## {{ record.title }} · `sec:toolchain:record`

**Decision (Compiler)** · `dec:toolchain:compiler` The workspace pins {{ policy.channel }} with {{ policy.components | join(" and ") }} under (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`). The dated compiler and its required components remain installed on every compute node while the repository names it; newer dated installs coexist with it. Gates and fuzz campaigns use this one named compiler. Move the pin only in its own gated commit, retaining the answering tool versions in the gate log.

**Decision (Minimum compiler)** · `dec:toolchain:minimum` The workspace and its first-party members declare no minimum compiler version. Tracking nightly would make a minimum a claim nothing checks, and no downstream consumer builds this workspace on an older compiler.

**Decision (Release profile)** · `dec:toolchain:release` Releases use {{ policy.lto }} LTO and {{ policy.codegen_units }} codegen unit. Thin LTO provides most of full LTO's cross-crate inlining at a fraction of its link time, and one codegen unit lets the optimiser see each crate whole. Dev and test profiles retain Cargo defaults so gate builds keep their incremental speed; changing their optimisation levels requires later measurement.

**Gate (Verification)** · `gate:toolchain:verification` The {{ policy.toolchain_gate }} step requires exactly {{ policy.channel }}, both declared components and answering compiler, Cargo, Clippy and formatter commands, and logs rustc -vV, cargo -V, cargo clippy -V and rustfmt -V. The {{ policy.profile_gate }} step refuses minimum compiler declarations in the workspace and first-party members and requires the reviewed profiles under (`amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile`). The {{ policy.tests_gate }} step witnesses acceptance of the pinned dated channel and rejection of the undated channel, another date, stable releases and missing components or command responses.

**Amendment (Development performance measurements)** · `amend:toolchain:development-performance` The current defaults and refusal of dev/test overrides remain the implemented policy until the foundation bite of (`gate:devperf:foundation`). That bite measures and decides the dev/test settings under (`dec:devperf:profiles`), then updates the profile declarations, checker and witnesses together to enforce the reviewed choices. This refines (`dec:toolchain:release`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`) without changing the compiler policy or the release profile. Build-time excess is a warning and an owned misconfiguration finding, never a failing gate verdict (`rule:devperf:builds`), (`rule:devperf:nonverdict`).

**Amendment (Measured gate profile)** · `amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile` This settles (`amend:toolchain:development-performance`) and refines (`dec:toolchain:release`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`) through the measured choice in (`amend:devperf:measured-choices`). {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.measurements', name='foundation-decisions').profile }} The build-policy checker requires exactly the reviewed release and gate profiles and continues to refuse dev/test overrides. Its witnesses reject changed optimization or symbols and disabled debug assertions or overflow checks. Gate Clippy, test, documentation and document-engine commands select the same named profile; timing findings remain separate from their semantic status.

**Amendment (Dated compiler)** · `amend:toolchain:dated-compiler` This amends (`dec:toolchain:compiler`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`): a retained dated install makes the compiler identity reproducible, while an undated channel can select a different compiler from the dated channel used by fuzz campaigns. Pinning the shared channel aligns those checks and makes a compiler move an explicit, gated change. The pin does not establish older-compiler compatibility or universal artifact reproducibility; (`dec:toolchain:minimum`) and the reviewed build profiles remain unchanged.
